  "query_preview": "..\n\nCooler north-northeasterly flow on the south end of a surface high \npressure migrating across Ontario and into Quebec tonight will \nensure more stable conditions locally. Limited cloud cover exists \nthis afternoon with the flow supplying a good amount of lower level \ndry air. The quiet weather expected for the remainder of the \nafternoon with seasonal afternoon high temperatures. \n\nUpstream over the western Great Lakes states, showers and isolated \nthunderstorms have developed on the leading edge of the theta-e \nridge and monsoonal moisture plume and at the nose of the low level \njet. Increasing cloud cover will arrive locally into the evening and \ntonight before greater rain chances begin to increase around 12Z \ntomorrow morning. Deeper moisture will be characterized by PWAT \nvalues climbing to 1.5 inches and above and will be focused along \nthe warm frontal slope over the course of the weekend. This supports \na good soaking rain and some isolated thunder potential Saturday \nmorning with additional rounds of heavy rain potential and isolated\nthunder Saturday night into Sunday. \n\nThe first round moving through Saturday morning with QPF amounts \ngenerally at 0.25\" to 0.75\" through 00Z Saturday evening. CAPE depth \nwill be lacking in a weak lapse rate environment with some hi-res \nguidance pointing toward a lull in heavier precipitation potential \nby mid afternoon Saturday given the showers moving across this \nweaker instability environment. Though, could still see scattered \nlight shower activity continue during the afternoon. MUCAPE will be \nminimally supportive of embedded thunder potential with isolated to \nscattered coverage that becomes lesser in the afternoon. Highest QPF \naxis is focused across southwest portions of the CWA in the latest \nHREF. \n\nThe next surge of monsoonal moisture along the frontal zone is \ndriven into the area Saturday after 00Z into Sunday morning. This \npresents the next chance for heavier precipitation potential with \nFGEN that could lead to areas of banded precipitation. Forecast QPF \nfrom 00Z Saturday to 12Z Sunday carries the highest 12 hour QPF \npotential with 0.75\" to 1.00\" or greater with the higher totals \ncovering smaller footprints based on where any focused banding sets \nup along the frontal slope.  \n\nLatest NBM has trended toward a more broad area of 1+ inches of \nstorm total precipitation over the course of Saturday and Sunday \ngenerally across the Tri-Cities east towards the Port Huron area as \na camp of models have moved this direction as opposed to a focused \nswath of higher precipitation across southeast portions of the CWA. \nStill lean towards the look of the HREF with highest precipitation \nfocused across southwest/southern portions of the CWA. Given the \nhigh end moisture and potential for repeated rounds, there will be \nat least a low chance for localized amounts of 2.00 to 2.50 inches \nover the 24 to 36 hour period. Given the spaced out nature of \nprecipitation and weak thunderstorm activity, widespread flooding \npotential looks limited, but will come with the typical urban \nflooding potential if repeated heavier rounds of precipitation do \noccur over these areas. \n\nAnother high pressure moves into central Ontario and then into \nQuebec late Sunday into Monday. This allows northeasterly flow to \npush bulk of any lingering showers activity to the southern MI \nborder or south. Cooler airmass with a tightening pressure gradient \nwill bring breezy conditions with temperatures dropping down into the \nlow to mid 60s for all of southeast Michigan for Monday and Tuesday. \nDeveloping trough mid-week will bring a return to more widespread \nrain precipitation chances early Wednesday.",
